PART C — STRICT NEW COOLDOWN POLICY

13. Exactly two special cooldown lengths

Implement a new global cooldown policy across the entire game.

The only permitted special cooldown lengths are 3 seconds and 5 seconds.

The existing universal 0.5-second repeat delay is excluded from this restriction and must remain intact.

No move may retain an arbitrary extra cooldown duration such as 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, or 12 seconds.

Apply the following rules universally.

Rule 1 — Ten-damage attacks: 5 seconds

Any attack or damaging move that deals 10 damage must have a 5-second cooldown.

This includes damage delivered through:

* Ordinary attacks.
* Projectiles.
* Techniques.
* Summoned entities.
* Multi-hit attacks with a 10-damage strike or finisher.
* Other character-specific damaging ability implementations.

Inspect the complete move definition, including nested hit, projectile, finisher, impact, and burst data.

Do not overlook a move simply because its 10-damage hit is delivered indirectly.

If a move has a defined total damage of exactly 10, account for that as well; don’t blindly assume its top-level damage field is always the relevant measure.

The cooldown applies to the move that the fighter activates, not separately to every projectile or hitbox created by it.

Rule 2 — Highly special abilities: 3 seconds

A move that provides a genuinely exceptional gameplay effect must have a 3-second cooldown, unless Rule 1 already requires 5 seconds.

Qualifying examples include:

* Direct healing or restoring Launch Point.
* Paralysis.
* A dedicated immobilizing effect.
* A special hard crowd-control effect that prevents normal character actions.
* Comparable major effects explicitly defined in the ability’s combat data.

For example, Unlimited Void’s paralysis qualifies.

Mystical Palm Technique’s healing qualifies.

Rule 3 — Ordinary abilities receive no extra cooldown

If a move does not qualify for Rule 1 or Rule 2, it must not have any additional cooldown beyond the universal 0.5-second repeat rule.

Do not assign a cooldown just because an attack:

* Looks powerful.
* Has impressive visual effects.
* Has a long animation.
* Launches enemies.
* Pushes enemies away.
* Pulls enemies inward.
* Homes toward a target.
* Creates an ordinary projectile.
* Causes ordinary hitstun or blockstun.
* Uses a standard quake, bounce, roll, or slam mechanic.

A pull effect is specifically not enough to qualify as highly special.

For example, character #0001’s Blue/Maximum Blue pulling behavior must not receive a 3-second cooldown merely because it draws opponents inward.

Likewise, ordinary combat hitstun should not qualify as special stun. Almost every attack has some hitstun; treating it as an exceptional mechanic would incorrectly give nearly everything a cooldown.

The 3-second category must be reserved for clearly exceptional effects such as actual paralysis, immobilization, and healing.

Rule precedence

When multiple conditions apply:

1. A qualifying 10-damage attack receives 5 seconds.
2. Otherwise, a highly special move receives 3 seconds.
3. Otherwise, the move gets no extra cooldown.

Never add the 3-second and 5-second cooldowns together.

Never stack special cooldowns.

The universal 0.5-second repeat behavior remains the game’s normal baseline and is not an additional special cooldown.

