15. Enforce cooldown policy centrally

The policy must be implemented in the shared combat/data architecture, not only in the three current character files.

Relevant systems include:

* js/data/cooldowns.js
* js/game/combat/attacks.js
* js/game/combat/technique.js
* js/game/combat/summon.js
* js/game/combat/combat-state.js
* js/game/fighters/fighter.js

Create or extend a shared cooldown classification/validation utility.

It must be capable of identifying qualifying damage or exceptional effects across direct attacks, projectiles, multi-hit strikes, techniques, and summons.

Prefer inferring policy from existing structured combat data.

Where inference cannot distinguish an extraordinary mechanic from a routine one, provide a carefully validated, generic semantic classification rather than making decisions from a move’s name or fighter ID.

Future fighters must follow the same policy automatically.

Don’t let a future character declare an arbitrary 8-second cooldown and silently bypass the rule.

Use validation or canonical derived values to ensure the final effective cooldown is permitted.

Preserve current cooldown lifecycle

Alva already distinguishes:

* Ordinary attack reuse timers.
* Technique and summon ability timers.
* Movement cooldowns and other movement-state restrictions.

Preserve the existing timing semantics unless a change is necessary to enforce the new policy.

Ordinary attacks currently begin their repeat cooldown when the attack ends or is interrupted.

Techniques and summons begin their cooldown when their activation is accepted.

Keep these behaviors consistent.

Do not accidentally make powerful abilities reusable by cancelling their startup animations.

Cooldowns must continue counting correctly during normal gameplay and character movement.

Do not alter combat damage, frame timing, hitboxes, launch calculations, hitstun durations, or animation sequences as a side effect of this rebalance.

These new rules concern move reuse, not the actual damage or functionality of attacks.

16. Single authoritative cooldown state

The HUD, player input validation, and combat AI must all agree on the same cooldown.

There must never be a situation where:

* The HUD says ready but the attack is on cooldown.
* The HUD says cooling down but gameplay allows the attack.
* The CPU uses an ability during a cooldown the player must obey.
* Switching from grounded to airborne resets a timer.
* A character swap leaves timers from the old fighter on the interface.

Create a shared read-only interface or helper for the UI to obtain:

* Move ID.
* Cooldown duration.
* Remaining time.
* Recovery progress.
* Ready/cooling state.

Use the existing CombatState.cooldowns and CombatState.abilityCooldowns as the authoritative sources.

The new HUD must not maintain duplicate gameplay timers.

