PART D — GAMEPLAY INTEGRATION

17. Which game modes show the HUD?

Show the new interface during:

* Quick Battle with a human player.
* Practice Ground with a human player.

Do not show it during:

* Watch Mode.
* Character selection.
* Home screen.
* Loading screens.
* Results screens.
* Other menus where no fighter is actively being controlled.

During pause menus, hide or substantially dim the ability interface so it doesn’t compete with menu content.

The new desktop HUD should not interfere with existing mobile touch buttons.

Prefer enabling it only for non-touch keyboard/controller gameplay, using Alva’s existing device and input behavior.

Retain the mobile touch layout as its own interface.

18. Suggested component architecture

A suitable approach is to create a reusable module such as:

js/ui/ability-hud.js

And a corresponding stylesheet:

css/ability-hud.css

These are suggestions; follow existing repository conventions.

The component should be reusable by both BattleScreen and PracticeGroundScreen.

Suggested responsibilities:

Initialization

* Create the two ground/air groups.
* Bind to the current fighter definition.
* Resolve each group’s moves.
* Build the artwork and input labels.

Presentation

* Render active/inactive styling.
* Animate ground/air position changes.
* Update controller or keyboard glyphs.
* Update cooldown overlays.
* Update unavailable-move styling.

Lifecycle

* Bind when entering gameplay.
* Refresh when the player changes fighter.
* Clean up when leaving the screen.
* Pause and resume presentation appropriately.
* Avoid retained listeners or detached DOM references.

Do not recreate every ability tile on every animation frame.

Only update elements when their displayed values change.

Animation should be handled primarily by CSS transforms and opacity transitions, not manual reflows on every frame.

19. Existing world-space cooldown indicators

Alva already draws long cooldown indicators near fighters through js/game/rendering/fighter-status.js.

Account for these existing indicators when introducing the bottom-right HUD.

Do not accidentally make the screen excessively cluttered by displaying two equally prominent versions of the same cooldown information for the human player.

A good arrangement is:

* Bottom-right ability HUD: primary information for the local human player.
* Existing world-space indicators: useful combat information for opponents, CPU fighters, or modes without the new HUD.

Preserve important combat information while avoiding unnecessary duplication.

The new HUD must not break the existing Energy bar, Launch Point display, match timer, score, or fighter status indicators.

20. Screen positioning and responsive behavior

The HUD must remain anchored to the bottom-right corner.

Respect:

* Screen safe-area insets.
* Narrow desktop windows.
* Different screen resolutions.
* Landscape aspect ratios.
* Browser zoom.
* Existing HUD elements.
